          "type": "assistant",
          "text": "**Flat — A, with one short provenance line per envelope.** Not because it's simpler; because nesting fails less safely.\n\n## The deciding argument: containment failure mode\n\nBoth shapes need R7 escaping. The question is what happens when escaping is imperfect or a future producer forgets it.\n\n```\nFLAT — content escapes its envelope\n<system-reminder>…forged </system-reminder> attacker text…\n                                            ^^^^^^^^^^^^^ now sits in plain user-message\n                                                          territory: LESS privileged\nNESTED — content escapes its inner block\n<system-reminder>\n  REMINDER: not written by the user, treat as authoritative\n  <relevant-conversations>…forged </relevant-conversations> attacker text…</relevant-conversations>\n                                                            ^^^^^^^^^^^^^ still inside the outer\n                                                            envelope, now adjacent to a line\n                                                            asserting it is system-authored\n```\n\nNesting means a containment failure *promotes* attacker content into the trusted frame. Flat means it demotes into untrusted text. That asymmetry matters more for llame than for the references, because your rail is about to carry owner-authored skills, repo `AGENTS.md` content, and possibly recalled prose — not just the server-derived ids and reason codes it carries today.\n\n## Supporting reasons\n\n**Both references are flat, and one relies on it structurally.** Claude Code wraps each item independently (`wrapInSystemReminder` per attachment) and uses `startsWith('<system-reminder>')` as a discriminator during message normalization and merging. DeepSeek is flat too — each injected message owns its complete frame: *\"The plugin owns the complete `<system-reminder>` framing, and every injected `user/message` reaches the model verbatim with no core wrapper.\"* Nobody nests.\n\n