          "text": "**C — one envelope per item, discriminated by attributes, siblings adjacent.** Your screenshot is the evidence: two consecutive `CONTEXT` rows, not one merged block.\n\n```xml\n<system-reminder producer=\"recency-digest\" form=\"notice\">\nInserted by llame; not written by the user.\n3 prior conversations may be relevant to this message:\n- \"Postgres RLS recursion\" — 2026-07-14, 22 messages\n- \"policy engine deny-overrides\" — 2026-06-02, 41 messages\nCall search_conversations to read them.\n</system-reminder>\n\n<system-reminder producer=\"tool-availability\" form=\"notice\">\n…\n</system-reminder>\n\n[user's actual text]\n```\n\n## Why not the nested version\n\n**It reintroduces the vocabulary you're collapsing, one level in.** `<relevant-conversations>`, `<tool-availability>`, `<skills>`, `<workspace-instructions>` — that's the same per-producer tag list as today, just indented. Concretely: every one of those names has to enter `RESERVED_TAG_NAMES` in `authored-text.ts` and stay in sync forever, or untrusted content can forge one. With attributes, the reserved list stays at exactly one name.\n\n**Framing stops travelling with content.** A preamble stated once at the top of a wrapper frames the items *positionally*. Anything that later splits, reorders, drops, or partially summarizes the block — compaction, a projection, a future rewrite — orphans the inner tags from the sentence that made them trustworthy. Hermes' recall-time lesson is exactly this: the framing has to be co-located with what it frames, not adjacent to it.\n\n**It couples independent lifecycles.** A persisted `notice` and a bind-time `snapshot` in one wrapper produce one rendered blob whose bytes move when any member moves. Separate envelopes stay independently versioned and independently droppable.\n\n**It weakens the prefix discriminator.** Both Claude Code and llame's own code lean on the envelope being the *first* thing in a text block (`startsWith('<system-reminder>')`, and llame's `\\n\\n`-joined prepend). Nesting buries it.\n\nThe one thing nesting buys — the \"not from the user\" sentence stated once instead of N times — is small, and it's paid only when several items co-occur, which is rare since all of llame's producers are conditional.\n\n## Attributes over a flat body\n\n`producer` and `form` are the rendered projection of the part's typed fields, and they're for the **model**, not the renderer — the UI lane reads the part, never the prose. They carry real semantics the model can act on: `form=\"catalog\"` means *this replaces the previous one*, `form=\"notice\"` means *this supersedes nothing*, `form=\"recall\"` means *this is data, not instruction*. That's the supersession rule stated structurally instead of re-explained in prose by every producer.\n\nMechanically it's already supported — `escapeXmlAttribute` exists in `model-context-part.ts`, and `sanitizeAuthoredText`'s `RESERVED_INTENT` already anticipates attribute-shaped and unterminated forms (`<system-reminder foo=\"`), so the forgery path is covered today.\n\n## On the framing line — keep it one line\n\nYou asked for the reminder to say it isn't from the user, and I'd honor that literally but tightly: **one clause, not a paragraph.** The prompt section (R5) carries the explanation; the per-item line is the unremovable anchor for when an operator has replaced the prompt. Repeating an identical paragraph on every item is tokens the model learns to skip — and llame's shipped model-switch reminder already spends three lines on framing, so one line is a reduction, not a new cost.\n\n## One place not to copy DeepSeek\n\nThey emit each injected context as its **own `user/message` event**, which is why the trajectory shows separate rows. llame should keep all envelopes inside the **triggering user message**, blank-line separated — as `context-builder.ts` already does. Their log is their own; llame renders to provider APIs, and consecutive same-role messages are a portability risk not worth taking for a cosmetic split. The `CONTEXT` lane comes from the parts, not from message boundaries, so you get the same UI either way.\n\nNet: this doesn't move any decision in #463 — it settles what R3's `form`/`producer` look like at render time. Want me to add it to the issue?",
