        {
          "type": "assistant",
          "text": "Good news: the separation you like is already in the data, not in the message boundaries. `data-model-context`, `data-tool-availability`, `data-recency-digest` are discrete, addressable, individually validated parts on the message — a trajectory viewer renders one row per part regardless of the fact that they concatenate into one `role: 'user'` string on the wire. You get DeepSeek's row-per-item transparency without the portability risk of consecutive same-role messages.\n\n## The catch a trajectory viewer exposes\n\n**The rendered prose is not persisted.** Every spec mandates that parts carry *semantics, not literal prose* — `renderModelSwitchReminder()` and friends generate the text at request-assembly time and throw it away. So a viewer claiming to show \"what the model saw\" has two options:\n\n- **re-render from the part at view time** — deterministic only if renderers are pure *and* versioned. Change a renderer's wording and every historical run in the viewer silently rewrites itself. That's a transparency tool that lies about the past.\n- **read it from the receipt** — which requires the receipt to record the *rendered* item, not merely that a part existed.\n\nThere's already precedent for the second: `model_context_snapshots.systemPrompt` stores the **rendered** prompt text, not the template plus inputs, precisely so an old run's prompt is recoverable after the template changes. The rail's rendered items are the missing analog of that column.\n\nSo this sharpens OQ4 twice over — receipt enumeration moves from \"nice, and blocking for content-bearing recall\" to **\"prerequisite for the viewer you want to build\"**, and it changes *what* it should store: rendered items, not references to parts.\n\n## And it probably answers OQ3\n\nI'd been framing the CONTEXT lane as something to bolt onto the chat transcript by generalizing the shipped model-switch boundary. Your viewer is the better home. DeepSeek splits it exactly this way — a **Chat** t