a second general-purpose subagent with the hostile-find-what-Codex-would-find framing (see \"Reviewer steering tactics\" above). Don't ship on a single reviewer.\n6. **Codex sandbox is read-only.** Codex can't write files, even if the prompt asks. Useful for adversarial verification (the reviewer can't accidentally apply a fix), but flag if the brief contains write actions — Codex will report inability to act rather than a content finding.\n\n## Verify Before Fix\n\nA reviewer pointing at a \"bug\" can be:\n- **Right** → apply the fix\n- **Wrong about the primary source** → cite back, don't apply, document the disagreement\n- **Right about the symptom but wrong about the cause** → apply a different fix targeted at the actual cause\n\n**Always verify load-bearing findings against primary sources before changing the document.** Especially:\n- Version numbers, arXiv IDs, DOI strings, paper titles, author lists\n- File paths, function names, API signatures, deprecation status\n- Units, sign conventions, coordinate frames, axis orientations\n- Numeric values, equation coefficients, table entries\n\nUse WebFetch on the abstract page. Grep the repo. Run the snippet under the project runtime. Don't apply a fix because a reviewer asserted it confidently — reviewers (including AI agents) hallucinate.\n\n## Audit surfaces — where bugs hide\n\nA normative spec fix in long-form prose often fails to propagate to parallel surfaces. Every spec-block fix needs a global grep + sweep of ALL these surfaces, not just the named fix site. The list grows monotonically as a loop proceeds — track new surface classes when they appear:\n\n1. **Equation prose** (math definitions in scientific notation)\n2. **Standalone math blocks** (rendered LaTeX-style formulas — frequently lag the prose paragraph above them)\n3. **Algorithm-step pseudocode** (imperative implementation steps that USE the equations)\n4. **Diagnostic / sanity-check code blocks** (helper functions; must match production selection-and-reduction logic exactly)\n5. **Worked-example narrative** (illustrative tables; \"the contribution is from the closest arm\" can survive after the equation prose was corrected to \"argmax(g_i)\")\n6. **Test brackets / asserted-field names** (the field the test asserts on must match the production-path field)\n7. **Recommendation prose** (top-of-doc summary; often the first thing readers see, and the last thing authors update)\n8. **Helper / dataclass docstrings** (return values, field semantics, parameter ordering)\n9. **Pedagogical caveats** (\"note that under v5's convention...\" — version-stamped notes rot)\n10. **Load-bearing constraint callouts** (arm-radius domain, clamp safety floors)\n11. **Derivation paragraphs** (paragraphs that USE parameters to compute bounds: N_LOS, memory, performance gates) — distinct from #1 because they CONSUME parameters\n12. **Narrative-justification paragraphs** (\"X is acceptable because Y\") — distinct from #11 because these don't derive, they only argue\n13. **Compact summary tables** (one-cell restatements of long-form prose — drift HERE persists even after the prose is corrected, because table-row revisions are batched separately from prose revisions and reviewer attention often slides past the table when the prose looks right)\n\nWhen fixing a normative claim, before declaring the fix complete, run `rg` for the corrected token across the whole document. Re-check every match against the surface class. If any class is missing from the grep result but plausibly affected, sweep that surface manually.\n\n### Arithmetic vs justification — audit both separately\n\nClasses #11 (derivation paragraphs) and #12 (narrative-justification paragraphs) are independently auditable. A 14-point arithmetic audit can pass cleanly (every numeric value computes correctly) while justification paragraphs still drift (the REASON given for choosing a number references stale premises). Don't treat them as the same audit.\n\nEmpirically: in observed loops where the arithmetic audit returned clean, ~30% of subsequent rounds still surfaced narrative-justification drift. The right derived bound can be presented with the wrong rationale, and reviewers focused on arithmetic don't catch it. Brief reviewers to audit both classes explicitly.\n\n### Convergence as surface-class exhaustion\n\nThe empirical convergence signal is NOT \"we ran enough rounds.\" It's \"the surface classes we've discovered are now all swept, and one full clean round confirms no NEW class has surfaced.\"\n\nTrack which audit-surface classes have produced findings. When the same classes recur for 2-3 rounds with diminishing-magnitude findings, and one round returns clean from both reviewers without surfacing a new class, the loop has exhausted its attack surface. Convergence is reached when the surface map stops growing AND the existing surfaces all return clean — not when an arbitrary round budget is hit.\n\nIf a new surface class surfaces late in a loop (e.g. \"compact summary tables\" appearing at round 47 after 6 prior rounds of parallel-callout findings), the loop is NOT converged. Add the new class to the audit checklist and run at least one more round.\n\n## Anti-patterns observed in practice\n\n### Heavy-rewrite anti-pattern\n\nA \"heavy rewrite\" — one version bump bundling 4+ substantive fixes — is ~2× more likely to introduce a new bug than five small per-version rewrites. The first verification round after a heavy rewrite should specifically scrutinize the rewrite, not just run the regression checklist.\n\nPrefer small per-version rewrites. If a heavy rewrite is unavoidable (rare), expect at least one fix-the-fix round.\n\n### Fix-the-fix bugs\n\nA precision fix can itself introduce a new precision drift. Example: rewriting \"D&S01 collapsed two scales into one knob\" to \"~3·N_LOS independent corrections\" got the per-LOS framing right but the arithmetic wrong (D&S01 picks ONE component per LOS, so ~N_LOS, not ~3·N_LOS). The fix